RISE ASCII SCENE 7
#############################################################################################################
#                                      RISE Ascii Scene File                                                #
#                                                                                                           #
#   File:           lathe_stress.RISEscene                                                                  #
#   Description:    A REGRESSION INSTRUMENT for `lathe_geometry`, not a demo.  Every specimen below is a     #
#                   case that has produced a bug or that the factory's own comments flag as delicate, laid   #
#                   out in two clean bands so a human can spot a regression BY EYE.  Companion to            #
#                   lathe_basic.RISEscene, which shows the three cases the chunk exists FOR; this one shows  #
#                   the cases that break it.                                                                #
#                                                                                                           #
#   LAYOUT: BACK band (z = -5), left to right --                                                            #
#                   COPPER horn / PLUM egg / SLATE spool / CLAY cutaway / GOLD pair                         #
#           FRONT band (z = +5), left to right --                                                           #
#                   JADE cup / AMBER pair / MAGENTA hourglass / TEAL tube / STEEL ziggurat                 #
#                                                                                                           #
#   SPECIMENS (what it stresses -> what CORRECT looks like):                                                #
#                                                                                                           #
#     MAGENTA hourglass, front band -- INTERIOR POLE.  THE MONEY CASE.  A symmetric peanut whose profile     #
#       touches the axis at r = 0 MID-WAY (0 -> 0.92 -> 0 -> 0.92 -> 0), so one pole vertex is shared by     #
#       two bands whose outward normals provably disagree in SIGN along the axis (lower band dr < 0 so       #
#       nh > 0; upper band dr > 0 so nh < 0), while a pole vertex's normal is forced to +-axis.              #
#       GUARDS the P1 in which that single shared vertex carried ONE normal and therefore sat in the         #
#       opposite half-space from one of its two fans -- `vGeomNormal` then pointed INTO the solid near the   #
#       pinch.  The factory now SPLITS an interior pole into two rows, one per band.                        #
#       CORRECT: the upper and lower lobes are shaded IDENTICALLY and symmetrically about the waist, and     #
#       the neck reads as a smooth pinch.  A REGRESSION shows as exactly ONE lobe going black (or blowing    #
#       out) in the band adjacent to the waist, breaking the up/down symmetry.                              #
#                                                                                                           #
#     JADE cup, front band -- POLE AT START ONLY.  r = 0 at the first profile point, r > 0 at the last:      #
#       closed dome at the bottom, OPEN rim at the top (a lathe emits no caps at 360).  CORRECT: a solid     #
#       rounded base, and the rim is genuinely open -- the baked mesh is double-sided, so the INNER wall     #
#       is visible and shaded through the mouth rather than showing a hole into the background.             #
#                                                                                                           #
#     PLUM egg, back band -- POLE AT BOTH ENDS.  A closed, watertight vessel: no caps, no zero-area          #
#       triangles.  CORRECT: a smooth egg with no seam and no dark speck at either pole.                    #
#                                                                                                           #
#     SLATE spool, back band -- HARD EDGES via DUPLICATED profile points.  The zero-length segment is not    #
#       "live", so it emits no band and splits the two normals.  CORRECT: three crisp creases (base disk /   #
#       barrel, barrel / waist, waist / collar) with smoothly shaded curvature BETWEEN them.  A regression   #
#       is either a uniformly rounded object (the split stopped happening) or a uniformly faceted one.      #
#                                                                                                           #
#     AMBER pair, front band -- `smooth TRUE` (left) vs `smooth FALSE` (right).  SAME profile, SAME          #
#       n_radial 12.  CORRECT: the LEFT one shades as a turned surface -- a continuous gradient around and   #
#       along the profile, with only the silhouette betraying the 12-gon.  The RIGHT one is visibly          #
#       faceted BOTH ways: 12 flat radial strips and a flat band per profile segment.  If the two look       #
#       alike, one of the two row-building branches has stopped doing its job.                              #
#                                                                                                           #
#     TEAL tube, front band -- TUBE / SHELL with LEVEL off-axis ends, at sweep_degrees 200.  Both profile    #
#       ends sit at h = 0 with r > 0, which is the `endsLevel` branch of the cap rule: closing the cross-    #
#       section THROUGH THE AXIS would put both axis projections on the SAME point, collinear with the two   #
#       endpoints, and ear-clip a zero-area spur.  The factory closes the cross-section DIRECTLY instead.    #
#       CORRECT: each of the two cut faces is a THIN WALL RECTANGLE (outer r 1.00 to inner r 0.72) and the   #
#       BORE IS OPEN -- the inner wall is visible the whole way through the C.  If a cut face is a solid     #
#       filled disk, or the object reads as a solid half-cylinder, the endsLevel rule regressed.            #
#                                                                                                           #
#     CLAY cutaway, back band -- PARTIAL SWEEP at 140 degrees on a SOLID silhouette (profile starts and      #
#       ends ON the axis), turned so the opening faces the camera.  Both cut half-planes get a flat          #
#       ear-clipped cap of the body's cross-section, and the two caps are wound OPPOSITELY (the start cap    #
#       faces -tangent(0), the end cap +tangent(sweep)).  CORRECT: both cut faces are opaque and lit; you    #
#       cannot see through the solid at any angle.  A cap wound the wrong way reads as a black gap.         #
#                                                                                                           #
#     GOLD pair, back band -- REVERSED vs FORWARD profile.  The same asymmetric profile authored bottom-to-  #
#       top (left) and top-to-bottom (right); SAME material, SAME transform but for x.  Outward orientation  #
#       is DERIVED from the signed volume of revolution, so authoring direction must not be load-bearing.    #
#       CORRECT: the two are INDISTINGUISHABLE.  If the right one is dark, inside-out, or reads as a         #
#       silhouette only, the volume-sign derivation regressed.                                              #
#                                                                                                           #
#     COPPER horn, back band far left -- `axis x`.  The axis frame (ax / au / av) was only ever pinned for   #
#       the default `y`.  CORRECT: the horn lies HORIZONTALLY along world X with the wide mouth at -X and    #
#       the closed tip at +X, circular in the Y/Z plane, and it is SHADED (a broken frame collapses the      #
#       revolution into a flat fan or puts the whole shape on the wrong axis).                              #
#                                                                                                           #
#     STEEL ziggurat, front band far right -- `axis z`.  A stepped cone revolved about world Z, so h runs    #
#       along +Z and the apex points at the camera.  Every step boundary is a duplicated profile point.      #
#       CORRECT: CONCENTRIC RINGS in the X/Y plane, five of them, each with a shaded annular tread and a     #
#       crisp riser edge; the rings are round and share one centre.  A broken axis frame turns them into     #
#       ellipses, a fan, or rings stacked along the wrong world axis.                                       #
#                                                                                                           #
#   No specimen here should produce a log line.  A clean render leaves RISE_Log.txt free of any `Error` or   #
#   `Warning` entry.  There is no ambient light on purpose: an inverted normal must read as BLACK, not as    #
#   a slightly darker grey.                                                                                 #
#                                                                                                           #
#   Author:         Aravind Krishnaswamy                                                                    #
#                                                                                                           #
#############################################################################################################

standard_shader
{
	name			global
	shaderop		DefaultDirectLighting
}

pixelpel_rasterizer
{
	max_recursion		10
	samples				16
	lum_samples			3
}

file_rasterizeroutput
{
	pattern				rendered/lathe_stress
	type				PNG
	bpp					8
	color_space			sRGB
}

film
{
	width			1500
	height			620
}

pinhole_camera
{
	location			0 13 32
	lookat				0 1.5 0
	up					0 1 0
	fov					17.0
}

